/// @file groups.dox
/// Doxygen-only file (not compiled) declaring ndl's top-level module groups.
/// Nothing here is C++; @ingroup tags elsewhere in the real headers attach
/// classes/functions to these groups so the generated docs have a "Modules"
/// index alongside the plain class list.

/// @defgroup core_image Core Image Types
/// Image<T,DIM>, its owning and bit-packed variants, and the zero-copy view
/// operations (view()/slice()/swap_axes()/mirror()) shared by all of them.
/// Image<T,DIM>'s own sum()/min()/max()/mean() reductions (whole-image, and
/// per-axis with a caller-provided keepdims output) have free-function
/// siblings of the same name in owned.h -- ndl::sum(src,axis)/min/max/mean --
/// that allocate their own OwnedImage<T,DIM> output instead, for the common
/// case where a caller just wants the result and doesn't already have a
/// buffer sized for it.

/// @defgroup morphology_filtering Morphology & Filtering
/// erode()/dilate()/median_filter()/percentile_filter()/threshold()/otsu_threshold()/
/// invert() -- free functions (morphology.h) over any minimal-interface image type
/// (Image or PackedBitImage), a toolkit built on top of Image rather than part of it --
/// plus the structuring-element helpers (make_box_kernel()/make_cross_kernel()) and
/// BorderMode. morphological_open()/morphological_close(), also here, are erode()-
/// then-dilate() and dilate()-then-erode() with the same kernel, collapsed into one
/// call (a caller-provided scratch buffer for the intermediate pass still required,
/// matching every other function here's "caller pre-allocates" convention).

/// @defgroup convolution Convolution
/// convolve()/gaussian_blur() -- free functions (convolution.h), a toolkit built on
/// top of Image rather than part of it, same relationship morphology.h's functions
/// have. Kept separate from Morphology & Filtering above: both groups walk a kernel
/// over every position, but convolution combines the values under it into a weighted
/// sum, where morphology picks a single one (min/max/percentile) -- a different
/// family of operation, even though they share BorderMode and the same kernel-tap
/// machinery underneath. downsample(), also here, is built on gaussian_blur(): blur
/// (so decimated-away pixels get averaged in, not aliased into noise) then keep every
/// Nth position along every axis except a caller-chosen channelAxis, returning a new,
/// already-sized OwnedImage. to_displayable() clamps a wider-range result (a kernel
/// with negative weights, a signed gradient magnitude) into a narrower directly-viewable
/// type's own range, optionally re-centered by a bias first. gradient() computes the
/// spatial gradient (one central-difference partial derivative per axis, via convolve()
/// itself) into a destination with an extra leading component axis -- the foundation
/// optical_flow.h's lucas_kanade_flow() is built on.

/// @defgroup visualize Visualize
/// bar_chart()/heatmap() (visualize.h) -- render any 1D/2D minimal-interface
/// numeric array as a real image (a bar chart or a greyscale intensity image
/// respectively), scaled to the array's own max value. Exists so nothing else in
/// this library needs its own one-off ASCII-art rendering: histogram_image()
/// (Histogram, below) is a thin dispatch straight to these, not separate drawing
/// code. percentile()/percentile_range()/windowed_heatmap(), also here, exist for
/// when that "scale to the array's own max" convention is the wrong call: a
/// single outlier value can otherwise dominate the whole displayed range, crushing
/// everything else in the image toward black. windowed_heatmap() scales to a
/// [lowPercentile,highPercentile] window instead (values outside it simply
/// saturate), and flow_to_color()'s own optional magnitudeCap parameter lets a
/// caller apply the same idea to a vector field's magnitude. flow_to_arrows(),
/// also here, is the other standard way to render a 2D flow field: a literal
/// arrow per sampled grid point (direction = angle, magnitude = length) rather
/// than flow_to_color()'s hue/brightness encoding -- composited directly onto
/// a caller-supplied background (a photo, a flow_to_color() image, or a plain
/// fill) rather than clearing it, and takes the same magnitudeCap convention
/// for the same outlier-robustness reason. flow_magnitude(), also here, is the
/// per-position Euclidean-norm reduction both flow_to_color()/flow_to_arrows()
/// use internally for their own auto-scaling -- unlike those two (genuinely
/// 2D-only: a single hue/arrow angle only ever encodes direction for a
/// 2-component vector), magnitude itself has no such restriction and works
/// for any component count and spatial dimensionality.

/// @defgroup channels Channels
/// reduceChannels()/channelReductionOutputCount() (processing/channels.h) -- collapse N
/// per-position channel values (RGB, or a trailing real+imag complex-as-channel-axis pair --
/// see FFT, below) down to 1 (grayscale) or 3 (true color) output values via a small named
/// set of modes ("rgb"/"magnitude"/"phase"/"sum"/"mean"/"max"). Independent of any image or
/// viewport machinery -- operates on a plain array of doubles -- so it's reused by both
/// viewer/viewport.h's own renderSlice()/renderVolume() (their own channelReduction param)
/// and the static browser viewer's own compositing (web/ndlviewer.js), rather than either one
/// hand-rolling the same reduction logic a second time.

/// @defgroup histogram Histogram
/// Histogram<VDIM>, histogram_equalize(), and histogram_image() (histogram.h) --
/// bins the joint distribution of VDIM co-occurring scalar values (VDIM==1 is the
/// ordinary single-channel histogram; VDIM==2/3/... are joint/co-occurrence
/// histograms), generalizing over VDIM the same way Image<T,DIM> generalizes over
/// spatial dimension DIM. otsu_threshold() (Morphology & Filtering, above) is
/// built on Histogram<1> internally; histogram_image() renders a Histogram<1>/
/// Histogram<2> as a real bar chart/heatmap via Visualize, above.

/// @defgroup distance_transform Distance Transform
/// distance_transform()/distance_transform_squared() (distance_transform.h) -- the
/// exact squared Euclidean distance transform (Felzenszwalt & Huttenlocher), computed
/// via one 1D lower-envelope-of-parabolas pass per axis, the same "one 1D pass per
/// axis" structure fftn() (FFT, below) uses. Always measures distance to the nearest
/// background (false/zero) position; invert() (Morphology & Filtering, above) flips
/// that to distance-to-nearest-foreground when that's what's wanted instead.

/// @defgroup summed_area_table Summed-Area Table
/// summed_area_table()/rectangle_sum() (summed_area_table.h) -- builds an integral
/// image via one prefix-sum pass per axis (again the same per-axis structure fftn()
/// uses), then answers "what's the sum over this arbitrary rectangle" in O(1) via
/// inclusion-exclusion over the table's own corners, regardless of the rectangle's
/// size. box_blur(), also here, is the payoff built on top: an O(1)-per-pixel
/// (2*radius+1)^DIM average via one rectangle_sum() query per pixel, with real
/// BorderMode support (Clamp/Wrap/Reflect, the same enum convolve()/erode() share) --
/// implemented by building one border-extended copy of the source up front (via the
/// same detail::kernelTapCoord() those use) so every downstream query lands on an
/// always-in-bounds table position, rather than by making rectangle_sum() itself
/// border-aware. box_filter_query(), also here, is rectangle_sum()'s variable-position/
/// variable-size sibling for an arbitrary fractional query center (Projection, below,
/// is the main consumer); rectangle_scatter_add()/box_filter_scatter_add() are their
/// exact adjoints -- not queries at all, but scatters into a delta array (the classic
/// D-dimensional difference-array technique: one summed_area_table() pass over the
/// accumulated deltas resolves every scatter at once), built to be the literal
/// transpose of what the query reads rather than assuming a box blur is a symmetric
/// operator (it isn't, right at an array's own boundary).

/// @defgroup optical_flow Optical Flow
/// lucas_kanade_flow() (optical_flow.h) -- dense per-pixel displacement estimation
/// between two frames, generalized to any DIM via the classic structure-tensor
/// formulation: a per-pixel DIM x DIM least-squares system (solved via
/// detail::solveLinearSystem()), whose windowed sums are themselves computed via
/// box_blur() (Summed-Area Table, above) rather than an explicit per-pixel window
/// loop. A flow field is an ordinary Image<double,DIM+1> with a leading component
/// axis of size DIM -- the same {channel,x,y} shape this library's color images
/// already use -- so it needs no flow-specific plumbing anywhere else in the
/// library. to_complex()/from_complex(), also here, convert a 2D flow field
/// to/from Image<std::complex<double>,2> specifically -- complex doesn't
/// generalize past 2D, but is a genuinely idiomatic 2D-only representation this
/// library already has full support for (FFT, above).
///
/// @defgroup features Feature Detection & Matching
/// detect_keypoints()/compute_descriptors()/match_descriptors()/sift_flow()
/// (feature_detection.h) -- a SIFT-*inspired* scale-invariant feature pipeline,
/// generalized to any DIM. The scale-space extrema detection (a Difference-of-
/// Gaussians pyramid built from gaussian_blur(), searched for extrema over the
/// full spatial+scale neighborhood) is a genuine, unambiguous N-dimensional
/// generalization of the real published algorithm. The descriptor is not: the
/// classic SIFT descriptor's 128-dimensional, 4x4-grid-of-8-orientation-bins
/// design has no natural N-D extension (published "3D SIFT" work invents an
/// entirely different descriptor, e.g. spherical histograms, rather than
/// mechanically generalizing the 2D one) -- so this library trades literal
/// fidelity to the published algorithm for genuine any-DIM support, using a
/// numCellsPerAxis^DIM grid of Gaussian-weighted, per-axis signed-gradient bins
/// instead of orientation histograms. Rotation invariance -- SIFT's own single
/// dominant-orientation ANGLE -- generalizes too, just not via an angle: any
/// N-D rotation decomposes into simple rotations of 2-D planes (the spectral
/// theorem), so compute_descriptors() instead aligns each keypoint's sampling
/// grid to the eigenbasis of its own local structure tensor (Matrix<double,DIM>,
/// eigen_decomposition() -- matrix.h), the same tensor lucas_kanade_flow()
/// (Optical Flow, below) builds densely. See compute_descriptors()' own comment
/// for the real, inherent (not bugs) loose ends this leaves: eigenvector sign
/// ambiguity (resolved via majority gradient projection) and instability at
/// degenerate/isotropic keypoints (which have no real preferred direction to
/// align to regardless).
///
/// @defgroup fft FFT
/// Complex/real power-of-two FFT (FFT, FFTReal), arbitrary-size FFT via Bluestein's
/// algorithm (FFTBluestein), and the N-dimensional Image-based entry points
/// (fftn()/ifftn()) most callers actually want. fftshift(), also here, is the
/// standard "center the spectrum" display convention: moves the zero-frequency (DC)
/// component from index 0 of each axis to that axis' own middle, wrapping the rest
/// around it -- fftn()'s raw output otherwise reads as meaningless noise until
/// recentered.

/// @defgroup matrix Matrix
/// Fixed-size, compile-time-dimensioned NxN matrices and the geometric transforms
/// (scale/translate/rotate/project) built on them, plus ProjectionMatrix<Real,D>
/// (matrix/projection.h) -- a D x (D+1) homogeneous matrix covering both
/// parallel-beam and cone/fan-beam projection geometry, and camera_center()/
/// ray_for_pixel(), which recover a projection matrix's own center of
/// projection (a finite source point, or a direction at infinity) and a
/// detector pixel's corresponding ray purely from the matrix itself, via its
/// null space (reusing this group's own SVD<Real,N>). What Projection, below,
/// is built on. fast_inverse(), alongside inverse() (matrix/decomposition.h),
/// is an adjugate/cofactor-based inverse (reusing determinant()/cofactor()'s
/// own direct N<=4 closed forms) for callers needing MANY inversions of
/// small, well-conditioned matrices -- ~3.6x faster than the general
/// SVD-based inverse(), at the cost of SVD's numerical robustness on
/// near-singular input; ray_for_pixel() and Projection's own per-sample
/// anti-aliasing footprint (below) are its two callers.
///
/// @defgroup fit Curve Fitting
/// polynomial_fit<Degree>() (fit.h) -- ordinary least-squares fit of a
/// degree-Degree polynomial over a sequence of (x,y) sample pairs, solved
/// by building the Vandermonde normal-equation system as a
/// Matrix<double,Degree+1> (in power-sum form) and reusing this group's own
/// SVD-based inverse(), rather than a hand-rolled solver per degree.
/// linear_fit()/parabolic_fit() are small, named-field (slope/intercept,
/// a/b/c) convenience wrappers over polynomial_fit<1>()/polynomial_fit<2>().
///
/// @defgroup interpolation Interpolation
/// Nearest/Linear/Quadratic/Cubic interpolator tags and sample()
/// (interpolation.h) -- reads a minimal-interface image at a fractional,
/// real-valued position via a separable weighted blend of its nearby
/// integer-indexed taps, border handling shared with convolve()/box_blur()
/// via detail::kernelTapCoord(). scatter_add(), also here, is sample()'s
/// exact adjoint (same weights, but adding a value INTO the nearby taps
/// rather than reading a blend of them OUT) -- what makes Projection, below,
/// able to implement forward/back projection as a genuinely (not just
/// approximately) matched operator pair.
///
/// @defgroup sampling Sampling
/// sample_image() (sampling.h) -- general N-to-N image resampling through an
/// arbitrary homogeneous Matrix<Real,DIM+1> transform (affine or projective),
/// backward-mapped (the transform maps destination coordinates to source
/// coordinates, the standard convention that avoids holes). Automatically
/// anti-aliases when downsampling: the transform's own Jacobian (exact, via
/// closed-form differentiation of the same perspective-divide
/// transform_point() itself performs, not a finite-difference
/// approximation) sizes a box prefilter from basic sampling theory, applied
/// via Summed-Area Table's box_filter_query() when the resulting footprint
/// would otherwise alias, skipped entirely (plain interpolation.h::sample())
/// when it wouldn't.
///
/// @defgroup projection Projection
/// forward_project()/back_project() (projection.h) -- CT-style projection
/// between a D-dimensional volume and a (D-1)-dimensional-per-view sinogram,
/// driven by one Matrix ProjectionMatrix<Real,D> (matrix/projection.h) per
/// view. forward_project() ray-marches (via ray_for_pixel()'s resolved ray)
/// and accumulates interpolation.h::sample()'d volume values into each
/// sinogram cell, a direct discretization of the Radon transform's line
/// integral; automatic anti-aliasing (see Sampling, above, for the general
/// idea) replaces that sample() call with summed_area_table.h's
/// box_filter_query() at each ray sample, sized from the view's own
/// Jacobian, when the volume's resolution would otherwise alias against the
/// detector's -- genuinely spatially-VARIABLE for cone/fan-beam geometry (a
/// voxel's footprint on the detector depends on its own distance from the
/// source), not just spatially-uniform the way Sampling's own affine-
/// transform case is. back_project() is built to be forward_project()'s
/// EXACT adjoint, not merely a good approximation of one (the more common
/// "voxel-driven gather" back-projector is not, despite looking similar) --
/// it re-walks the identical rays via the same detail::planRaySamples(),
/// scattering each sinogram value back with the same per-sample weight
/// forward_project() reads it with: interpolation.h::scatter_add() (the
/// exact adjoint of sample()) on the unfiltered path, or
/// summed_area_table.h::box_filter_scatter_add() (the exact adjoint of
/// box_filter_query()) when autoAA is on -- matched at the level of the
/// per-sample read/write itself, not a separate blur stage bolted on
/// afterward, which is what keeps the two exact adjoints of each other
/// (verified via a dot-product test, <forward_project(x),y> ==
/// <x,back_project(y)>, not just asserted -- unitTests/projection_tests.cpp
/// checks both the unfiltered AND the AA-enabled path, including data
/// touching the volume's own boundary, all to ~1e-14 or tighter) whether or
/// not autoAA is engaged. Both parallelize over views via std::execution::par
/// when a parallel STL backend is available (TBB, the same optional
/// dependency fft.h's fftn()/ifftn() already use) -- forward_project() is
/// embarrassingly parallel (disjoint sinogram rows per view); back_project()
/// chunks views across hardware_concurrency() workers, merging each chunk's
/// own accumulated contribution into the shared volume once per chunk
/// (mutex-protected) rather than once per view. max_density_bound(), also
/// here, derives a per-voxel upper density bound directly from a measured
/// sinogram (the non-negativity assumption real CT reconstruction already
/// relies on, applied to forward_project()'s own Riemann-sum structure) --
/// clamping an iterative reconstruction to [0, max_density_bound(sinogram)]
/// each iteration ("projected" Landweber/gradient descent, demo/
/// ct_reconstruction and demo/ct_reconstruction_3d both do this) is what
/// keeps background voxels the sinogram shows a clear line of sight through
/// from drifting into the bright overshoot artifacts unconstrained
/// iteration is prone to -- and, for an underdetermined system (fewer
/// detector measurements than volume unknowns), prevents the semi-convergence
/// (RMSE improving then later worsening) plain unconstrained Landweber is
/// prone to there. compute_camera_centers(), also here, precomputes
/// camera_center() for every view up front; forward_project()/back_project()
/// each accept the result via their own `centers` overload (their plain
/// geometry-only overload still computes it internally, once per view, every
/// call) -- worth it for an iterative reconstruction loop that calls either
/// function repeatedly with the same geometry, since camera_center() is an
/// SVD and otherwise gets redundantly resolved every single iteration.

/// @defgroup image_io Image I/O
/// Loading and saving images in bitmap/PNG/JPEG/DICOM/NRRD/raw formats.

/// @defgroup utilities Utilities
/// Small standalone helpers (Timer, code_timer(), change_endian()) not specific
/// to image processing.

/// @defgroup viewer N-D Viewer
/// pairwise_slice<AxisA,AxisB>()/for_each_axis_pair<DIM>() (viewer.h) -- zero-copy
/// 2D views of every pairwise-axis plane through an Image<T,DIM>, generalizing the
/// classic axial/coronal/sagittal synchronized triple view to any DIM and axis pair.
/// normalize_to_u8()/write_web_volume() export a volume in the small binary format
/// web/ndlviewer.js (a standalone, dependency-free WebGL viewer embedded directly
/// into the generated tutorial pages -- see docs/generate_tutorial.py) reads to
/// render and interactively cursor-sync every pairwise view in a browser.
